that hole mobility in spiro-OMeTAD is negligibly small (probable because its time-of-flight mobility is $2\times10^{-4}~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})^{33}$ and that hole transfer from PVK to HTL occurs very quickly (also probable because time constants of 16 ns for $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{2}C l^{34}}$ and ca. 10 ns for two-step $\mathrm{PVK}$ have been observed), we can deduce that the hole is the primary charge carrier contributing to the TRMC mobility in PVK $(\mu_{+}=17\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\mu_{\scriptscriptstyle-}=3\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{'}\mu_{+}=19\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{-}}=0.6\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{-}1}\mathrm{s}^{\mathrm{-}}$ 1). These assumptions are validated by the decay profiles, which show that the HTL coating significantly quenched the PVK conductivity on a long timescale $(>\mathsf{c a.}\mathrm{~1~}\upmu\mathrm{s.}$ , Figure 1b). Furthermore, such a low electron mobility is consistent with the equal $\Sigma\mu$ values that were observed for PVK and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ despite the reported instantaneous electron injection from PVK into $\mathrm{\mp{-}T i O}_{2}$ .25 Notably, the $\mu_{-}$ in $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and PVK (0.6 and $3~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ) shows good consistency with the electron mobilities of 0.66 and $1.39\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ reported for pure PVK on glass, on the basis of fluorescence lifetime spectroscopy studies by Snaith et al.6 and Xing et al.,7 respectively.35 Despite the unbalanced mobility $(\mu_{+}/\mu\approx30)$ and its inferior $\mu$ , the $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ device fabricated by the two-step method worked efficiently. In sharp contrast, $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ had more comparable mobilities with $\mu_{+}=7~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\mu_{\scriptscriptstyle-}=3\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . Due to its electron transport capability, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ offers a bypass for electrons to travel towards the cathode, which can compensate for the low electron mobility in the perovskite phase. Conversely, electrically-inert mp${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ can neither receive nor transport electrons, the perovskite crystallites must, therefore, contact the underlying substrate through the jungle-like mesoporous alumina. This situation, therefore, certainly favors complete conversion of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ to PVK in the deep mp- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ region, which might be more suited to a one-step procedure than a two-step procedure. In addition to the different contribution of $\mu_{+}$ and $\mu_{-}$ dependent on the underlying scaffold, the transient conductivity of Spiro/PVK/TiO2 decays slower than those of Spiro/PVK and Spiro/PVK/Al2O3, which is relevant to the improved device output of PVK/TiO2.
## 2.2 Crystalline Quality Assessment by X-ray Diffraction and morphology
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra were measured (Figure 2) to obtain crystallographic information about the perovskite on the mesoporous scaffolds. The spectra were clearly resolved for $2\theta=10{-}45^{\circ}$ , and the peaks were readily assigned to PVK $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3})}$ ), $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ according to the literature.3,10,36 Hereafter, we concentrated our attention on the two peaks at the low diffraction angles of $2\theta=12.7^{\circ}$ (due to the (001) plane of the hexagonal 2H polytype phase of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ ) and $14.1^{\circ}$ (due to the (110) plane of the tetragonal phase of PVK).10 Although both of these peaks appeared in all of the spectra of the samples containing PVK, their relative peak intensities varied with changes to the underlying mesoporous layer. The $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak at $12.7^{\circ}$ was more pronounced in the $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ spectra, where it was about 2–3 times higher than the PVK peak at $14.1^{\circ}$ . Conversely, the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak was less pronounced (only about half as high) than the PVK peak in $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . However, the amplitude of the PVK peak in $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was comparatively small, despite the substantially-reduced $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ peak. This indicates that more unconverted $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ remained in the $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ samples than in $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . On the contrary, those samples prepared by one-step method completely quenched the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peaks irrespective of the scaffold, leaving only the PVK peaks. Nonetheless, the solar cells based on one-step method have a lower PCE and reproducibility3 with a large hysteresis on voltage sweep37,38. The crystal size correlating with the slow polarization of perovskite was proposed as a possible cause of hysteresis by Park et al., where the hysteresis was mitigated by the increase in crystal size and the use of mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .39
Atomic force microscopy (AFM) of our perovskite indicates that the crystal size of two-step $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(420\pm50~\mathrm{nm})$ is obviously larger than those of one-step $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $300\pm80~\mathrm{nm})$ ) and both $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $(70\mathrm{~-~}100\mathrm{~nm})$ as shown in Figure 3a-3e. Importantly, Figure 3f presents a general correlation with the minimum mobility $(\Sigma\mu)$ at 9 GHz and the crystal size, irrespective of one- and twostep methods and mesoporous scaffolds. The crystal size can be also controlled by tuning the PbI2 concentration.40 Our finding exemplifies the relationship between inherent charge carrier mobility (mainly hole) and crystal size of perovskite affected by the preparation process and under layer. It is of interest to note that one-step $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ contains both large and small crystals (Figure 3d). Electronic
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
property of nano-sized semiconductor such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles is often susceptible to the trapping at surface defects and inter-structural barrier for charge transport, which are reduced with increasing the crystal size.31 In this work, the two-step $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ having the largest size showed the highest TRMC mobility and PCE. The analogy infers that the crystal size relating to the polarization of perovskite,39 rather than incomplete conversion of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , limits the TRMC mobility and overall device output.
Along with a respectable $15\%$ PCE, Burschka et al. demonstrated that mp- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ facilitates the conversion of $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ to the perovskite during the two-step process, because of confinement of $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ within the nanoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ network.10 They observed almost complete extinction of the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peak in the XRD spectra of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . In contrast, their PVK sample without mp- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ generated intense $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ peaks. The conversion of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to PVK starts immediately after immersing a $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}/\mathrm{mp}/\mathrm{iO}_{2}$ film into a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ solution, initially on the surfaces of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ crystallites but quickly proceeds towards the inside layer of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ at the mp- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface. However, it is commonly recognized that prolonged immersion in the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ solution deteriorates the resulting devices photovoltaic performance.42 Therefore, as discussed above the remaining thin $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ layer on the mp- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ surface after a short immersion (e.g., 20 s) does not crucially block the electron transfer from PVK to mp-TiO2. It is noted that $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ on its own produces a TRMC signal, but the intensity of this signal is more than two orders of magnitude lower than that of PVK. This is in agreement with the photocurrent/photovoltage results on $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , in which the diffusion constant of charge carriers on a long range (film thickness) is a factor of 5 smaller than that in perovskite.43 Meanwhile, computational study suggests that the formation of the flat terminations under the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ -rich condition leads to the improvement of device performance, benefited from the shallow surface state for hole transfer.44 This is also a reasonable interpretation on the coexistence of the highest hole mobility and remaining $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ in our $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . However, an ideal morphology would be a dense and flat perovskite layer with large uniform domain and minimal remaining $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ , which was achieved via vapor deposition by Snaith et al. with $15.4\%$ PCE.9 Recently, Soek et al. have realized such a flat perovskite layer by solvent engineering, leading to the remarkable PCE of $16.2\%$ with no hysteresis.45
