whole $I_{0}$ range, highlighting the presence of a small population of shallowly-trapped charge carriers. It should be noted that the $E_{\mathrm{T}}$ of these shallowly-trapped carriers was less than $0.3\mathrm{eV}$ , typically about 0.01 to $0.12\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and deeply-trapped charges $\mathrm{\Delta}E_{\mathrm{T}}>0.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ are too immobile to contribute to the GHz conductivity. The $\vec{\tau^{\prime}}\vec{\slash{\tau}}$ ratio of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ decreased to 0.8, on average, concomitant with a weak decreasing tendency with increasing $I_{0}$ . Note that for pure PVK, the $\vec{\tau^{\prime}}/\vec{\tau}$ ratio had a strong convex dependence on $I_{0}$ . The $\vec{\tau^{\prime}}\vec{\slash{\tau}}$ ratio was close to 1 at low $I_{0}$ ( $10^{10}$ photons $\mathrm{cm}^{-2\cdot}$ ), it then rapidly decreased to a minimum of 0.7 at $10^{12}$ photons $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and finally increased to 0.8. Interestingly, this convex trend is similar to the relative intensities of $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ between pure PVK and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as shown in Figure 1a. Although they both exhibited a $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ of $20\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at the lowest $I_{0}$ , the $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ of PVK decreased more rapidly than that of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with increasing $I_{0}$ . The $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ of PVK was reduced to as much as $40\%$ of that of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . This difference was slightly mitigated at high $I_{0}$ where it was reduced to $50-$ $60\%$ . From this observation, we expect the charge carrier mobility in pure PVK was greatly impaired by the presence of shallowly trapped states, which resulted from the poor formation of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ crystallites on a flat substrate.10 A similar scenario has been suggested in another report, which indicated that the presence of a thin mesoporous interlayer is responsible for reducing the exciton binding energy of the perovskite and increasing the surface effects (structurally and electronically) by tuning the perovskite crystal size.51
2.5 Frequency Modulation up to 23 GHz: High Mobility and Shallow Trap Depth
Finally, we performed frequency-modulated TRMC studies at 9, 15, and $23\mathrm{\GHz}$ . Figure 6a shows that $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ increased greatly with increasing frequency, rising by a factor of three to six (all kinetics are given in Figure S5). At $23~\mathrm{{GHz}}$ under the lowest $I_{0}$ excitation, the $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ values of PVK, $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ were between $60{-}75~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (see the inset of Figure 6a and Table 1). The notable increase in the minimum mobility with increasing frequency was mainly due to an increase in $\phi,$ which was probably constant at low $I_{0}$ because of the improved time resolution. This was strongly corroborated by the flat dependence of $\phi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ on $I_{0}$ at low $I_{0}$ values, which is more obvious at $23\:\mathrm{GHz}$ than at 9 GHz. Emphasis should be given to the observation that the high TRMC mobility measured for PVK correlates with the electron mobility of a polycrystalline PVK sample $(66~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ estimated by Hall effect measurements at room temperature.52
Moreover, we observed low $f_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values for PVK and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(0.03\pm0.01)\$ and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (0.08 $\pm\ 0.03)$ . Interestingly, the $f_{\mathrm{trap}}$ of PVK at $0~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ increased to $0.1\pm0.02$ at intermediate $I_{0}$ $7.3\times10^{12}$ photons $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , Table 1), which coincided with the $I_{0}$ that resulted in the minimum $\vec{\tau^{\prime}}\vec{\slash{\tau}}$ ratio, as shown in Figure 5. These results imply that the local mobility of shallowly-trapped charge carriers is strongly modulated by the charge carrier density. $E_{\mathrm{T}}$ and $f_{\mathrm{trap}}$ fluctuated only within the limits of experimental error at each delay time, suggesting an absence of apparent trapping and/or selective recombination processes on a long timescale.32 These results support the electronically robust nature of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , in
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
sharp contrast to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $(f_{\mathrm{trap}}=0.8\ –0.98)$ and P3HT:PCBM $(f_{\mathrm{trap}}=0.25)$ .32 The conductivity transient maxima of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ at low temperatures shown in Figure 7 revealed a $T^{1.4}$ dependence (all kinetics are given in Figure S6), clearly indicating band-like transport rationalized by phonon scattering $(T^{3/2})$ . Although PVK has a phase transition in the investigated temperature region (being orthorhombic below $162~\mathrm{K}$ and tetragonal from 162 to 327 K),53 no significant change in the slope was found. Our results were consistent with similar TRMC measurements on $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (with $T$ 1.7) reported by Savenije et al.54 The indicated band-like transport is in accordance with the observed high local mobility and negligibly small $E_{\mathrm{T}}$ and $f_{\mathrm{trap}}$ . Therefore, the excellent photovoltaic properties of $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are ensured, in a synergetic manner, by their high charge carrier mobility with band-like transport, low charge recombination rate, low trap density, and shallow trap depth.
## 3. CONCLUSION
In conclusion, we investigated the dynamics and local transport properties of charge carriers in sequentially deposited $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite samples using the TRMC technique. The minimum mobilities at 9 GHz were determined for pure PVK and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(20~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for both), and for $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $(10\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . On the basis of conductivity quenching by spiro-OMeTAD, we separated the contributions of holes and electrons in $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (19 and $0.6\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{PVK}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (7 and $3\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-}$ $^1\mathrm{{s}^{-1}}$ ). The minimum mobilities, $\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{min}}$ were found to generally correlate with the crystal size (70 – 420 nm) evaluated by AFM, irrespective of the process (one or two-step) and underlying layer $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ ). Remarkably, high mobilities of $60{-}75~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ were revealed at $23~\mathrm{{GHz}}$ in these composites and explained by band-like transport with a $T^{1.4}$ dependence due to phonon scattering. Charge recombination in PVK was suppressed by 4–5 orders of magnitude from the Langevin rate, and was further reduced by another order of magnitude in the presence of a mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer. Analysis of the GHz complex conductivity revealed a very low $E_{\mathrm{T}}$ of $10\ \mathrm{meV}$ and $f_{\mathrm{trap}}$ of 0.03–0.1 in these PVK frameworks. In addition to the prominent optoelectronic properties of PVK itself, these values were
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
improved by the use of a mesoporous layer, which provided a scaffold on which electronically highquality crystalline PVK, with a high mobility, a reduced recombination rate, and a low trap density, could be formed. More specifically, whether the scaffold is ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ determines the mobility and dynamics of charge carriers and the energetic landscape of the trap state.
## EXPERIMENTAL
Materials. mp-TiO2 (300 nm-thick) or mp- ${\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ (220 nm-thick) samples were prepared on a cleaned quartz substrate by spin-coating a diluted $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ paste, followed by sintering at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a furnace. A perovskite layer was formed either on these mesoporous layers or directly on the quartz by sequential deposition (two step). First, an $N{-}N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) solution of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ $(99.999\%$ trace metals basis) was spin-coated and immediately annealed at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30 min. Second, the $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ films were dipped into an isopropanol (IPA) solution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ for $20~\mathrm{s}$ , followed by rinsing in fresh IPA for 2 s and annealing at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The resultant thicknesses of PVK were 260 and $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ on mp- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and mp- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ , respectively. A one-step PVK on the similar mp- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ or mp- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ was prepared by spin-coating totally $40\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ DMF solution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (1:1 molar ratio) followed by heating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $45~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Subsequently, ca. $150~\mathrm{nm}$ -thick HTL was deposited on the two-step perovskite/mp-TiO2(or mp- $.\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{,}$ )/quartz samples by spin-coating a chlorobenzene solution of 2,2’,7,7’-tetrakis(N,N-di- $p$ -methoxyphenyl-amine)9,9’-spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD), 4-tertbutylpyridine (TBP), and lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl) imide salt (LiTFSI). Further details of the solution contents and preparation conditions including devices are given in the Supporting Information. AFM observations were performed by a Seiko Instruments Inc. model Nanocute OP and Nanonavi II. XRD spectra were measured on a Rigaku MiniFlex600 (Cu Kα: 1.5418 Å) X-ray diffractometer.
Transient Conductivity. TRMC experiments were carried out using the third harmonic generation (THG; $355~\mathrm{nm}$ ) of a Nd:YAG laser (Continuum Inc., Surelite II, 5-8 ns pulse duration, $10~\mathrm{Hz}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ) as an excitation source. X-band (ca. 9.1 GHz), $\mathrm{K_{u}}$ -band (ca. 15.4 GHz), and K-band (ca. 22.9 GHz) microwave circuits equipped with their respective resonant cavities were used to probe the GHz conductivity of all samples. The increase in the reflected microwave power and the negative shift of the resonant frequency of the cavity corresponded to the positive real and negative imaginary conductivities, respectively. Details of this system and the complex conductivity measurements have been previously reported.32 The experiments were performed at room temperature in air.
